    "description": "The inlay button with the hover moved onto the rim: two plates, one inside the other, and a solid rim on the inner one's edge. The outer plate is the button's real box and carries only a fill; the inner one is the same shape inset by 2px on every side, so the chip keeps the size scale's outside dimensions and the label sits exactly where the matte button's label sits. A continuous rim needs no SVG, so it is a real outline pulled back onto the face by a negative offset, which keeps it off the 2px gutter and out of the inner plate's box, leaving the label's padding untouched. Both faces hold still under the pointer, because the rim is the whole event: a plate that changes underneath it competes with the line for the eye, and the line is the thinner of the two signals. The rim comes forward in both themes, but it cannot do that by going one direction in both, since light draws a mid grey face, where forward is a near-white glint, and dark draws a pale one, where the same glint would vanish and forward is ink instead. One value at rest, two at hover. Sizes and the leading icon come from the matte button, which means they all sit on the same scale and take path data, an image src or a lucide glyph alike.",
