    "title": "Fancy Button",
    "summary": "The inlay button with a pixel field laid over its face: two plates, one inside the other, a solid rim on the inner one's edge, and a lattice of five pixel crosses tiled across the plate inside it.",
    "description": "The inlay button with a pixel field laid over its face: two plates, one inside the other, a solid rim on the inner one's edge, and a lattice of five pixel crosses tiled across the plate inside it. The outer plate is the button's real box and carries only a fill; the inner one is the same shape inset by 2px on every side, so the chip keeps the size scale's outside dimensions and the label sits exactly where the matte button's label sits. The field is a mask rather than a picture, a 12px tile punched out of a plate filled with the page's own background token at 10 percent, which is the one value that reads on both faces, since these plates run opposite the page: dark in light mode and pale in dark, so the page fill is light against the first and dark against the second. One rule, no second value for the other theme. Under the pointer the field halves that opacity and nothing else moves with it, so the pattern recedes as the label brightens. It sits on its own element rather than as a second background layer on the face, because a mask applies to the whole box and would take the plate fill with it, and the label rides above the field on a positioned wrapper so the crosses never pixel the glyphs. The tile holds at 12px through every size, so the chip grows around the field rather than scaling it. Sizes and the leading icon come from the matte button, which means they all sit on the same scale and take path data, an image src or a lucide glyph alike.",
