    "title": "Pinstripe Button",
    "summary": "The fancy button with its pixel field swapped for a ruled column: two plates, one inside the other, a solid rim on the inner one's edge, and a 1px line every 3px down the plate inside it.",
    "description": "The fancy button with its pixel field swapped for a ruled column: two plates, one inside the other, a solid rim on the inner one's edge, and a 1px line every 3px down the plate inside it. The outer plate is the button's real box and carries only a fill; the inner one is the same shape inset by 2px on every side, so the chip keeps the size scale's outside dimensions and the label sits exactly where the matte button's label sits. The rule is one repeating gradient rather than a masked SVG tile, because vertical lines are what a gradient already draws: no asset to load, no mask, and nothing that could take the plate fill with it. It is drawn in the label's own ink rather than a colour of its own, so the two greys of the source artwork are gone and the plate, the rim and the line all read out of the theme instead. How far back the rule is held is an opacity on the element it sits on, 15 percent at rest and half of that under the pointer, so the columns drop back as the rim and the label come forward. The label rides above the rule on a positioned wrapper so the lines never stripe the glyphs, and the 3px period holds through every size, so the chip grows around the rule rather than scaling it. Sizes and the leading icon come from the matte button, which means they all sit on the same scale and take path data, an image src or a lucide glyph alike.",
