    "title": "Sheen Button",
    "summary": "The pinstripe button lit from both edges: two plates, one inside the other, a solid rim on the inner one's edge, a 1px line every 3px down the face, and a fade running in from the top edge and in from the bottom over the top of it.",
    "description": "The pinstripe button lit from both edges: two plates, one inside the other, a solid rim on the inner one's edge, a 1px line every 3px down the face, and a fade running in from the top edge and in from the bottom over the top of it. The outer plate is the button's real box and carries only a fill; the inner one is the same shape inset by 2px on every side, so the chip keeps the size scale's outside dimensions and the label sits exactly where the matte button's label sits. The fade is two linear gradients on one element, both stopped short of the middle so a clear band survives across the centre instead of the two stacking there and lighting it brightest, which would be the opposite of the shape. Both the rule and the fade are drawn in the label's own ink: these plates run opposite the page, dark in light mode and pale in dark, and the ink follows them, so one value lights the face in both themes with no per-theme twin to keep in step. Hover is the inlay button's rather than the pinstripe's, the plate itself brightening while the rule and the fade hold where they are, so the chip reads as something lighting rather than something switching off. Sizes and the leading icon come from the matte button, which means they all sit on the same scale and take path data, an image src or a lucide glyph alike.",
